[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186068714 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" "question-e70442d3-8437-54dd-ba25-74163c48588a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/id #uuid "a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-60172" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x+4y=1$ and $2x-2y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 2x-2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-4y$ term that can cancel with the $4y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 &  \\\\ 2x-2y=3 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 4x-4y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ (3x+4x) & =(1+6) \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=1$ into the equation $2x-2y=3$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-2y & =3 \\\\ 2-2y & =3 \\\\ -2y & =1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee62bf3c-cdf7-51b8-b6f8-f8df94a25f98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-239b4a9b-56e4-5a86-8a68-9c399e4eaa37" "answer-ee62bf3c-cdf7-51b8-b6f8-f8df94a25f98" "answer-7014a716-ba56-5a31-b1a8-377a659ba2df" "answer-95309e0e-fd50-5c5e-b095-3c20e548ce32" "answer-082e1fb8-38d1-5dd8-8994-bb44202783c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-239b4a9b-56e4-5a86-8a68-9c399e4eaa37" :answer/id #uuid "239b4a9b-56e4-5a86-8a68-9c399e4eaa37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee62bf3c-cdf7-51b8-b6f8-f8df94a25f98" :answer/id #uuid "ee62bf3c-cdf7-51b8-b6f8-f8df94a25f98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7014a716-ba56-5a31-b1a8-377a659ba2df" :answer/id #uuid "7014a716-ba56-5a31-b1a8-377a659ba2df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95309e0e-fd50-5c5e-b095-3c20e548ce32" :answer/id #uuid "95309e0e-fd50-5c5e-b095-3c20e548ce32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-082e1fb8-38d1-5dd8-8994-bb44202783c0" :answer/id #uuid "082e1fb8-38d1-5dd8-8994-bb44202783c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No intersection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068715 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ea2f44ab-dddd-5fb6-b2ba-34d59e15c7bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea2f44ab-dddd-5fb6-b2ba-34d59e15c7bc" :question/id #uuid "ea2f44ab-dddd-5fb6-b2ba-34d59e15c7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-60182" :question/problem "The lines $y=3x-b$ and $y=x+5$ intersect at the point $C$ with coordinates $(a,2).$ Find the parameters $a$ and $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(a,2)$ is the point of intersection of the two lines, the values $x=a,\\,y=2$ must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-b \\\\ y=x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=a,\\,y=2$ to obtain a new system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=3a-b \\\\ 2=a+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =a+5 \\\\ -3 & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=-3$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3a-b \\\\ 2 & =3(-3)-b \\\\ 2 & =-9-b \\\\ 11 & =-b \\\\ -11 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $b=-11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-799a5806-fdb3-56b9-abdb-217c2f17b6fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-799a5806-fdb3-56b9-abdb-217c2f17b6fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "799a5806-fdb3-56b9-abdb-217c2f17b6fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-011c4ed3-b08e-5bb4-8d64-714af50167c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-602e967c-a929-567a-9257-15cb77561af6" "answer-011c4ed3-b08e-5bb4-8d64-714af50167c9" "answer-156f3b79-c1f4-564c-9343-71d9780571e6" "answer-46ace411-49e7-5267-8ce7-27b0b740cb4a" "answer-6a59c9dc-7269-58a8-99a5-540b40f347b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-602e967c-a929-567a-9257-15cb77561af6" :answer/id #uuid "602e967c-a929-567a-9257-15cb77561af6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-011c4ed3-b08e-5bb4-8d64-714af50167c9" :answer/id #uuid "011c4ed3-b08e-5bb4-8d64-714af50167c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-156f3b79-c1f4-564c-9343-71d9780571e6" :answer/id #uuid "156f3b79-c1f4-564c-9343-71d9780571e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=0,\\,b=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46ace411-49e7-5267-8ce7-27b0b740cb4a" :answer/id #uuid "46ace411-49e7-5267-8ce7-27b0b740cb4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a59c9dc-7269-58a8-99a5-540b40f347b9" :answer/id #uuid "6a59c9dc-7269-58a8-99a5-540b40f347b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=4,\\,b=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e70442d3-8437-54dd-ba25-74163c48588a" :question/id #uuid "e70442d3-8437-54dd-ba25-74163c48588a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75873" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $2x+y=12$ and $x-2y=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then we will get a $2y$ term that can cancel with the $-2y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=12 & |\\,\\times 2 \\\\ x-2y=6 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y=24 \\\\ x-2y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\cancel{2y} & =24 \\\\ x-\\cancel{2y} & =6 \\\\ (4x+x) & =(24+6) \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=6$ into the equation $x-2y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y & =6 \\\\ 6-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=6,\\,y=0.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(6,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-906a9694-dc14-5b8b-9fcf-596a282ca62d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-906a9694-dc14-5b8b-9fcf-596a282ca62d" :answer-field/id #uuid "906a9694-dc14-5b8b-9fcf-596a282ca62d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-146b7b45-9602-56bf-9589-403ec424c06f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-08848abb-4fdd-54a8-a134-d62409d65dd8" "answer-a20276d7-269b-565c-a947-89053ba18c09" "answer-146b7b45-9602-56bf-9589-403ec424c06f" "answer-7f322004-2d74-5c3e-ab36-7b710e50faaf" "answer-93ad6313-5531-5f5e