b93d7-4708-529a-b82a-6f35378c3f1c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cedb93d7-4708-529a-b82a-6f35378c3f1c" :step/id #uuid "cedb93d7-4708-529a-b82a-6f35378c3f1c" :step/content "question-e70442d3-8437-54dd-ba25-74163c48588a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/id #uuid "a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af"] ["question-a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-60172"] ["question-a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the lines $3x+4y=1$ and $2x-2y=3.$"] ["question-a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 2x-2y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then we will get a $-4y$ term that can cancel with the $4y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 &  \\\\ 2x-2y=3 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+4y=1 \\\\ 4x-4y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\cancel{4y} & =1 \\\\ 4x-\\cancel{4y} & =6 \\\\ (3x+4x) & =(1+6) \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=1$ into the equation $2x-2y=3$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-2y & =3 \\\\ 2-2y & =3 \\\\ -2y & =1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"] ["question-a1dae329-b51c-5003-8d13-154486e1f8af" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4432d3-c731-55d8-bd24-6b2c198e1059"] ["answer-239b4a9b-56e4-5a86-8a68-9c399e4eaa37" :a